  "body": "### What is the issue with the HTML Standard?\n\nIn the navigation API's [resolve the finished promise](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/nav-history-apis.html#resolve-the-finished-promise) steps, it includes the following:\n\n> Resolve apiMethodTracker's [committed promise](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/nav-history-apis.html#navigation-api-method-tracker-committed) with its [committed-to entry](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/nav-history-apis.html#navigation-api-method-tracker-committed-to-entry).\n\n> Usually, [notify about the committed-to entry](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/nav-history-apis.html#notify-about-the-committed-to-entry) has previously been called on apiMethodTracker, and so this will do nothing. However, in some cases [resolve the finished promise](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/nav-history-apis.html#resolve-the-finished-promise) is called directly, in which case this step is necessary.\n\nI don't believe this is accurate and think the step and note should be removed. From what I can tell, resolve should never be called without the commit steps having already been run. If not, the `committed-to` entry would still be `null` unless there is some place that sets it without doing the other steps in [notifying about the committed-to](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/nav-history-apis.html#notify-about-the-committed-to-entry) and I don't think that's the case.",
